/**
@mainpage haidi_bms_cpp

An asynchronous, transport-agnostic driver for HAIDI battery management systems
speaking the CAN/485/UART protocol V4.0.

The library never touches a serial port, a clock or a thread. It encodes
requests, frames and decodes replies, and hands results back through callbacks;
moving bytes and measuring time are the caller's job.

@section mainpage_wiring Wiring it up

Four things connect a haidi::HaidiBMS to the outside world:

| | |
|---|---|
| haidi::HaidiBMS::set_tx_handler  | invoked whenever bytes must go out on the wire |
| haidi::HaidiBMS::set_event_handler | invoked whenever a reply, timeout or error occurs |
| haidi::HaidiBMS::feed            | called with bytes as they are received |
| haidi::HaidiBMS::tick            | called periodically with a monotonic millisecond count |

@code{.cpp}
using namespace haidi;

HaidiBMS bms{HostAddress::COMP};
bms.set_tx_handler(&on_tx, &fd);
bms.set_event_handler(&on_event, &app);

bms.poll_total_voltage_current_soc();   // returns immediately

for (;;) {
    bms.tick(monotonic_ms());
    uint8_t buf[64];
    const ssize_t n = ::read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);
    if (n > 0) {
        bms.feed(buf, static_cast<size_t>(n));
    }
}
@endcode

Results arrive as a haidi::Event. Read the payload with the checked accessor for
the command you asked for; it returns @c nullptr if the event is anything else,
so there is no need to trust haidi::Event::id by hand:

@code{.cpp}
void on_event(void*, const Event& event) {
    if (const auto* v = as_total_voltage_current_soc(event)) {
        printf("%u.%u V\n", v->cumulative_voltage_dv / 10, v->cumulative_voltage_dv % 10);
    }
}
@endcode

Any poll may also carry its own handler, invoked before the session-wide one:

@code{.cpp}
bms.poll_capacity_voltage(&on_capacity, &app);
@endcode

@warning haidi::HaidiBMS::tick is not optional bookkeeping. Sending and
receiving are clock-free, so a link on which every reply arrives appears to work
without it -- but deadlines are examined nowhere else, so a single lost reply
stalls the session permanently, and paginated reads whose length is unknown
never complete at all.

@section mainpage_layers Layers

Each layer is usable and testable on its own:

| Layer | Header |
|---|---|
| Frame codec: constants, Data IDs, checksum, big-endian readers | haidi_protocol.hpp |
| Byte-stream framer: resynchronising, checksum-verifying | haidi_frame_parser.hpp |
| Decoded payload structs, one per command family | haidi_payloads.hpp |
| The event type and its checked accessors | haidi_event.hpp |
| The session: queue, timeouts, reassembly, dispatch | haidi_bms.hpp |

@section mainpage_protocol Protocol notes

Every frame is exactly 13 bytes in both directions, and the bus is strictly
host-polled: the BMS never speaks unsolicited, and section 5.3 of the protocol
document states that "two data items cannot be read at once", so the session
keeps at most one request outstanding and queues the rest.

Correlation is by the echoed Data ID alone -- there is no sequence number -- and
the protocol defines no error or NAK reply. The only failure signals available
are a timeout, a bad checksum, a malformed frame, a reply from the wrong
address, a mismatched Data ID, and for the two MOS control commands an echoed
state that differs from the one requested.

Several of those are reported while the request stays in flight, so one
request can produce more than one event: a corrupted frame is reported, and the
request is still answered, retried or timed out afterwards. haidi::ErrorCode
says which errors end a request.

The normative reference is @c docs/CAN @c 485 @c UART @c Protocol @c V4.0 @c HAIDI.pdf
in this repository. Where its own annotations are self-contradictory, the
affected declarations carry an @c \@note recording the reading that was chosen
and why.

@subsection mainpage_pagination Paginated replies and the frame counter

A reply longer than eight bytes is split across frames, with Byte0 of each
carrying a frame counter and Byte1 onwards the payload. The base that counter
starts from is not uniform: the document specifies 1 for 0x55, 0x56, 0x57 and
0x6A, and 0 for 0x62, 0x63, 0x64, 0x95 and 0x96.

**Devices do not reliably honour it.** A pack has been observed answering 0x95
with frames numbered 1, 2, 3 where its own document specifies 0 — verified from
a byte-level capture, checksums intact:

@verbatim
A5 01 95 08 | 01 0B B8 0B B8 0B B8 00 | 8D
A5 01 95 08 | 02 0B B8 0B B8 0B B8 00 | 8E
A5 01 95 08 | 03 0B B8 0B B8 0B B8 00 | 8F
              ^^ frame counter
@endverbatim

The session therefore treats the documented base as a default, not as fact. Each
frame is filed under the counter the device actually sent, and the base of a
transfer is taken to be the lowest counter that arrived. Both conventions decode
correctly on every paginated command, and no configuration is involved.

Two consequences worth knowing:

- A counter of 16 is legal. The widest reply the protocol allows is 0x95 with
  48 cells over 16 frames, which on a pack counting from 1 numbers them 1..16.
- A reply is truncated at the first **missing** frame rather than returned at
  full length with a hole in it. A hole in 0x95 would be indistinguishable from
  cells genuinely reading 0 mV, so the count comes back short instead — which,
  as below, is not an error.

A paginated reply that stops early is reported as a successful response carrying
a smaller count, never as a failure: every paginated payload is self-describing,
and for 0x95 and 0x96 on a pack whose cell count has never been read, the
inter-frame timeout is the *normal* end-of-reply signal rather than an error
path. The exception is the 0x64 fault-record stream, which has no length of its
own: one that goes quiet before its 0xFF end sentinel is reported as
haidi::ErrorCode::TRUNCATED.

